<!doctype html><html lang="en-GB"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Source navigation · Umicom Framework</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../assets/site.css"><script defer src="../assets/site.js"></script>@UMICOM_SITE_ICONS@</head><body><header class="top">@UMICOM_SITE_BRAND@<nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="index.html">Wiki</a><a href="../source-map.html">Source map</a></nav></header><main id="content"><h1>Finding your place in a source file</h1>
<p>A compiler message may point to line 12 of a file. A colleague may ask you to inspect a statement on line 40. Instead of scrolling and counting, you can ask the editor to move directly to that location.</p>
<p>In Umicom Studio, the toolbar, the Navigate menu and Ctrl+G use the same Framework navigation service. The service moves the <strong>caret</strong>—the point where new text would be inserted. It does not change the programme or save a file.</p>
<nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents"><a href="#practice">Try it in Notes</a><a href="#positions">Lines and columns</a><a href="#contracts">Use the contracts</a><a href="#example">Complete example</a><a href="#files">Find the implementation</a><a href="#checks">Check the result</a></nav>
<h2 id="practice">1. Move to a line in Umicom Notes</h2>
<p>Open a disposable Umicom Notes project in Studio and open its C source file. The following small programme is useful for practising navigation. Its braces and indentation make it easy to recognise each line.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
int main(void)
{
    puts("Umicom Notes");
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Save the file once so that you have a known starting point. Choose <strong>Navigate → Go to Line…</strong>, or press <strong>Ctrl+G</strong>. Enter <code>4:5</code> and choose <strong>Go to location</strong>. The caret should move to the <code>p</code> in <code>puts</code>: line 4 begins with four spaces, so the next byte is column 5.</p>
<p>The editor toolbar also accepts <code>4:5</code> in its <strong>Line:column</strong> field. Entering only <code>4</code> moves to the beginning of line 4. The form's Enter key submits the location. <strong>Cancel</strong> closes the form without moving the caret or clearing a selection.</p>
<p>Now add an unsaved comment above <code>puts</code>. Request the line containing that statement again, using its new line number. Navigation reads the <em>current editor draft</em>, not an older saved copy. Your comment remains unsaved until you choose Save.</p>
<h2 id="positions">2. Understand a source position</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Input</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>The beginning of the first line.</td></tr><tr><td><code>4:5</code></td><td>Line 4, UTF-8 byte column 5.</td></tr><tr><td><code> 4:5 </code></td><td>The same position. Spaces and tabs around the whole input are allowed.</td></tr><tr><td><code>0</code>, <code>-2</code>, <code>+2</code>, <code>4:0</code></td><td>Invalid: both numbers must be positive and contain digits only.</td></tr><tr><td><code>4 : 5</code> or <code>4:5:2</code></td><td>Invalid: the separator must be one colon, without internal spaces.</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Lines and columns start at 1. A line that does not exist is rejected, leaving the caret where it was. A column beyond a real line's end moves to that line's end. This makes a short line usable without inventing spaces. An empty line is still a line; a final newline also creates an empty last line.</p>
<h3>A byte is not always a displayed character</h3>
<p>Umicom's existing source-position contract counts UTF-8 <strong>bytes</strong>. The letters in <code>ABC</code> each use one byte. In <code>A£B</code>, however, <code>£</code> uses two bytes: <code>A</code> starts at column 1, <code>£</code> at column 2 and <code>B</code> at column 4. A request for column 3 is moved back to the beginning of <code>£</code>, so the caret cannot split its encoding.</p>
<p>A tab character occupies one byte even when it is displayed as several spaces. These columns therefore differ from screen-cell or grapheme columns. When another tool uses a different convention, its adapter must convert the position before calling this contract.</p>
<h3>Keep the intended document</h3>
<p>A location form records the identity of the active document when it opens. If application code selects another tab before the form is submitted, the old request is refused. It must not move the caret in that other file. Cancel the old form and open a new one for the intended document.</p>
<p>The form is modal during normal interaction. The identity check also covers changes made by other commands or application callbacks. It checks the active document's identity, not an immutable copy of its text: edits within the same document are resolved against its current draft. Closing the original document or replacing the editing binding also invalidates the request.</p>
<h2 id="contracts">3. Use navigation in another Umicom application</h2>
<p>A <strong>contract</strong> describes what a function accepts, what it promises and who owns its data. The public navigation header is <code>umicom/document/navigation.h</code>. Link a native C application to <code>Umicom::document</code>; the GTK form additionally uses <code>Umicom::ui_gtk4</code>.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Contract</th><th>Purpose and example</th></tr><tr><td><code>UmiDocumentLocation</code></td><td>Contains the one-based <code>line</code> and <code>column</code>. The example parses <code>4:5</code> and prints both values.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UMI_DOCUMENT_LOCATION_TEXT_MAX</code></td><td>Limits input to 96 bytes, checked before reading it. This bounds the request text, not the size of an open document.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiDocumentLocationParse(text, byteCount, outLocation)</code></td><td>Reads exactly the supplied bytes. No allocation occurs, and a failed parse leaves the caller's output unchanged. A buffer need not end in a zero byte when its length is supplied correctly.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiDocumentCoordinatorNavigate(documents, expectedDocument, text, byteCount, outOffset)</code></td><td>Parses the request, checks the active document ID and delegates to the existing <code>UmiDocumentCoordinatorGoToPosition</code>. The optional output is a zero-based byte offset in the complete draft.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiGtk4AdapterDocumentNavigate(adapter, location)</code></td><td>Uses a zero-terminated location string for an immediate native request. It reports the result through the current document-edit completion binding.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiGtk4AdapterPromptDocumentLocation(adapter)</code></td><td>Opens or presents the shared form. Returning OK means the form opened, not that the user navigated. Go reports a result; Cancel does not perform navigation.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiGtk4AdapterDocumentHasCompletion(adapter)</code></td><td>Lets the host decide whether the current binding will display the command result. It is a read-only query, not a command or a guarantee that a later binding remains alive.</td></tr></table></div>
<p>The registered command keeps its existing identifier, <code>umicom.document.go-to-line</code>, and accepts the same <code>line</code> or <code>line:column</code> syntax. Existing numeric-line callers do not need a new command ID. In Studio, invoking the menu without an argument opens the input form; a command with an argument navigates immediately.</p>
<h3>Keep ownership explicit</h3>
<p>The caller owns its input buffer and output structure. The parser does not retain either. The navigation function borrows the coordinator for the call. Capture the active document's nonzero ID before collecting input and keep the coordinator alive until that operation ends.</p>
<p>All document and GTK calls run on the document owner's thread. The GTK form retains an invalidatable binding token rather than owning the coordinator. Removing the binding closes the form and stops retained controls from applying an old request. No background worker, document registry or Undo stack is created for navigation.</p>
<h3>Read the result before proceeding</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Result</th><th>What to do</th></tr><tr><td>OK</td><td>For direct navigation, the position was applied. For form creation, wait for the user's action.</td></tr><tr><td>Invalid Argument</td><td>Check the syntax and use positive decimal numbers. Embedded zero bytes, signs and numeric overflow are rejected.</td></tr><tr><td>Capacity Exceeded</td><td>Keep the location request within 96 bytes.</td></tr><tr><td>Not Found</td><td>Check that a document is open and that the requested line exists.</td></tr><tr><td>Invalid State</td><td>The original document is no longer active. Ask for a fresh location in the correct tab.</td></tr><tr><td>Unavailable</td><td>Connect the native adapter to the existing document coordinator before asking it to navigate.</td></tr><tr><td>Out of Memory</td><td>Keep the current draft intact and retry when resources are available.</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Read-only documents allow navigation because moving a caret does not alter their text. Successful navigation clears the current selection; a rejected request leaves it unchanged.</p>
<h2 id="example">4. Build the complete example</h2>
<p>The following programme creates its own temporary Notes source file. It opens that file through Framework, requests <code>4:5</code>, then proves that input captured for one document cannot navigate a different active tab. Finally, it checks both the complete draft and saved bytes. It removes only the temporary directory it created.</p>
<pre><code>@UMICOM_SITE_NAVIGATION_EXAMPLE@</code></pre>
<h3>Follow the code</h3>
<p><code>commands</code>, <code>workbench</code>, <code>store</code> and <code>documents</code> are separate owners with different responsibilities. The command registry names operations. The workbench contains the document views. The document store holds the working content, and the coordinator connects the existing services.</p>
<p><code>TRY</code> checks each return value and sends a failure to the cleanup section. The cleanup section releases resources in reverse order. It is reached on both success and failure, so an early error does not skip the release steps.</p>
<p><code>intended.document_id</code> records which document the first request belongs to. The second tab intentionally changes the active document. The next call returns Invalid State, and the example checks that the second tab remains active. It then reads the original draft and the saved file independently; the printed message alone is not the test.</p>
<h3>Run it from an Applications development build</h3>
<p>After configuring the Applications build, compile the example target. These commands use the existing Windows preset:</p>
<pre><code>Set-Location "C:\umicom\Umicom-Applications"
$env:Path = "C:\msys64\ucrt64\bin;$env:Path"
&amp; "C:\msys64\ucrt64\bin\cmake.exe" --build --preset windows-ucrt64-all-debug --target umicom-document-navigation-example --parallel 2
&amp; ".\build\windows-ucrt64-all-debug\bin\umicom-document-navigation-example.exe"</code></pre>
<p>On Linux, after configuring the existing headless development preset:</p>
<pre><code>cmake --build build/headless-debug --target umicom-document-navigation-example --parallel 2
./build/headless-debug/bin/umicom-document-navigation-example</code></pre>
<p>The expected output is:</p><pre><code>Requested line 4, UTF-8 byte column 5.
The caret points to puts; navigation did not edit the source.
A different active tab rejected the old request and remained active.
The Notes draft and saved file are unchanged.</code></pre>
<h3>Use an installed Framework SDK</h3>
<p>The same source directory includes a CMake project using <code>find_package(UmicomFramework CONFIG REQUIRED)</code>. Set <code>CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH</code> to your Framework installation. For example, on Linux:</p>
<pre><code>cmake -S framework/examples/editor_workflow -B build/notes-sdk -G Ninja -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="$HOME/umicom/installed/framework-sdk"
cmake --build build/notes-sdk --parallel 2
ctest --test-dir build/notes-sdk --no-tests=error --output-on-failure</code></pre>
<p>Its navigation executable is <code>umicom-notes-navigation</code> (with <code>.exe</code> on Windows). The other editor examples remain in this project, so the test command also checks them.</p>
<h2 id="files">5. Find the implementation</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Location</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr><tr><td><code>framework/include/umicom/document/navigation.h</code></td><td>The public location type, limit, parser and guarded navigation contracts.</td></tr><tr><td><code>framework/src/document/commands.c</code></td><td>The shared parser, active-document guard and existing registered command. The old parser remains commented beside its replacement.</td></tr><tr><td><code>framework/src/document/coordinator.c</code></td><td>The existing complete-draft position operation. Navigation reuses it rather than adding another text store or position engine.</td></tr><tr><td><code>framework/include/umicom/ui/gtk4/document_commands.h</code></td><td>The native prompt, direct request and completion-query contracts.</td></tr><tr><td><code>framework/adapters/gtk4/document_commands_gtk4.c</code></td><td>Creates the GTK form, manages its binding token and reports early command failures through the existing completion route. <code>LocationPromptClose</code> invalidates a retained form immediately.</td></tr><tr><td><code>framework/src/application_shell/profiles/navigate_menu.c</code></td><td>The shared Go to Line menu contribution.</td></tr><tr><td><code>applications/studio/src/gui/workbench/runtime/interaction/editor_controls.inc</code></td><td>The toolbar delegates its location input to Framework. Earlier Undo/Redo and navigation code remains commented with migration notes.</td></tr><tr><td><code>applications/studio/src/gui/workbench/runtime/runtime_helpers.inc</code></td><td>Uses Framework's Edit-command availability and checks for an active source document before enabling navigation.</td></tr><tr><td><code>applications/studio/src/gui/workbench/runtime/interaction/command_router.inc</code></td><td>Routes immediate input or opens the shared form; it does not parse a second location syntax.</td></tr><tr><td><code>applications/studio/src/gui/workbench/runtime/runtime_actions.inc</code></td><td>Uses the existing completion binding for error feedback and retains the unbound-route fallback.</td></tr><tr><td><code>applications/studio/src/gui/workbench/runtime/profiles/keybindings/go_to_line.inc</code></td><td>The Ctrl+G contribution included by the existing catalogue.</td></tr><tr><td><code>framework/tests/document_editing/test_document_navigation.c</code> and <code>test_document_navigation_gtk4.c</code></td><td>Parser, document, command and native-form cases. The fixture demonstrates setup and cleanup for a complete GTK host.</td></tr></table></div>
<h2 id="checks">6. Check the operation in Studio</h2>
<p>Use the same file through the toolbar, Navigate menu and Ctrl+G. Check the caret position and the full text after each request. Try an invalid number, a missing line, a long column and a read-only document. Open the form and cancel with a selection present: the selection should remain.</p>
<p>Keep a second draft open. Navigation must not save it or move its caret by mistake. Use the existing <strong>Report problem</strong> tools to record the exact starting document, request and result when something differs. Screenshots show where the caret appeared; a saved-file comparison checks that navigation did not write the file.</p>
<p>The form exposes the automation targets <code>document.location.window</code>, <code>document.location.input</code>, <code>document.location.message</code>, <code>document.location.go</code> and <code>document.location.cancel</code>. Tests should inspect the resulting document state, not just whether a control received a click.</p>
<p>Source navigation reads the complete supported draft synchronously. It is not a background search. On large files, copying and resolving that text can take time. The existing document size and ownership limits still apply.</p>
</main><footer>Umicom Foundation</footer></body></html>